RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
##########################################################################
# ARBITRATION STARVATION DEMO
#
# Setup: 4 requesters ALL ask for ONE shared resource EVERY cycle.
# The resource has a single port, so exactly one request is granted per cycle.
#
# STARVATION = the resource works and others keep getting it, but one requester
# never does. 'max_wait' == total cycles means 'never granted at all'.
##########################################################################
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
==========================================================================
 POLICY: FIXED priority (req 0 always wins)
==========================================================================
  Rule : the lowest index always wins.
  Risk : requester 0 asks every cycle, so requesters 1..3 are never even looked at.
         This is what 'a high-priority requester always wins' does to everyone else when
         that requester bids every cycle.
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] narrating the first 12 cycles (waits = cycles since last grant)
RPT_WARNING @ 100 [ARB] requester 1 has now waited 100 cycles without a grant: STARVATION suspected
RPT_WARNING @ 100 [ARB] requester 2 has now waited 100 cycles without a grant: STARVATION suspected
RPT_WARNING @ 100 [ARB] requester 3 has now waited 100 cycles without a grant: STARVATION suspected
RPT_ERROR @ 1000 [ARB] requester 1 was NEVER granted in 1000 cycles: STARVED
RPT_ERROR @ 1000 [ARB] requester 2 was NEVER granted in 1000 cycles: STARVED
RPT_ERROR @ 1000 [ARB] requester 3 was NEVER granted in 1000 cycles: STARVED
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO] RESULT after 1000 cycles (4 requesters, 1 grant/cycle):
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 0: served=1000 max_wait=0   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 1: served=0 max_wait=1000   <-- STARVED (never granted)
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 2: served=0 max_wait=1000   <-- STARVED (never granted)
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 3: served=0 max_wait=1000   <-- STARVED (never granted)
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
==========================================================================
 POLICY: ROUND-ROBIN
==========================================================================
  Rule : a pointer rotates over the requesters, each gets its turn.
  Gain : perfectly fair, the wait is bounded by (requesters - 1) cycles.
  Cost : no priority at all - an urgent requester (e.g. the oldest request) waits like the rest.
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] narrating the first 12 cycles (waits = cycles since last grant)
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO] RESULT after 1000 cycles (4 requesters, 1 grant/cycle):
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 0: served=250 max_wait=3   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 1: served=250 max_wait=3   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 2: served=250 max_wait=3   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 3: served=250 max_wait=3   ok
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
==========================================================================
 POLICY: FIXED + AGING
==========================================================================
  Rule : fixed priority, BUT a requester that has waited >= the age limit (8 cycles) is
         promoted ahead of the fixed order (longest waiter first).
  Gain : priority is kept (requester 0 still gets the most grants) and nobody starves:
         the wait is bounded by roughly (age limit + requesters).
  Watch: from cycle 9 on, requesters 1..3 age out one after another and get their grants.
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] narrating the first 12 cycles (waits = cycles since last grant)
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO] RESULT after 1000 cycles (4 requesters, 1 grant/cycle):
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 0: served=668 max_wait=3   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 1: served=111 max_wait=8   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 2: served=111 max_wait=9   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO]   requester 3: served=110 max_wait=10   ok
RPT_INFO @ 1000 [DEMO] 
TAKEAWAY: starvation is a property of the SELECTION RULE, not of the resource.
A rule that can pick the same winner forever (fixed priority) starves the rest;
round-robin or aging puts an upper bound on how long anyone waits.

